-- title:
--   Galois conjugate of σ equals σ twisted by a diamond
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ (as a `Fact`), an $M$ with $M \neq 0$ and $p \nmid M$, and a field $L$ of characteristic zero which is a $\{p\}$-cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, together with $\zeta \in L$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((q))$ equal to [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103), the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image under [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of the $q$-expansion function field of $\Gamma_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, and let $j \in K$ have Laurent series [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81). Let $\sigma$ be an $L$-algebra automorphism of $K$ satisfying the generator law: there are a ring embedding $\iota : L \to \mathbb{C}$ and $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M) \cap SL_2(\mathbb{Z})$ with $p \mid \gamma_{11}$ such that for every weight $k$, all forms $f, g$ of weight $k$ on $\Gamma_1(Mp)$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f, p_g$ (i.e. $p_f$, $p_g$ mapped to $\mathbb{C}$ are the $q$-expansions of $f$, $g$), with [`ModularCurve.intSeriesC ℚ pg ≠ 0`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L69), and every $x \in K$ whose Laurent series is `coeffEmb L` of $p_f/p_g$, there exist forms $\varphi_f, \varphi_g$ of weight $k$ on $\Gamma_1(Mp)$ and non-zero integers $D_f, D_g$ with $\varphi_f = D_f \cdot \big(\tau \mapsto (f\mid_k\gamma)(\mathrm{heckeDiagMatrix}\,p \cdot \tau)\big)$ and likewise for $\varphi_g$, each of $\varphi_f$, $\varphi_g$ a finite $\iota(L)$-linear combination of forms possessing integral $q$-expansions, and such that in $\mathbb{C}((q))$ one has $\iota$ applied coefficientwise to $\sigma x$, times $D_f$ times the $q$-expansion of $\varphi_g$, equals $D_g$ times the $q$-expansion of $\varphi_f$. The conclusion: for every $s \in \mathrm{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ and $b \in \mathbb{N}$ coprime to $p$ with $s\zeta = \zeta^b$, every $b'$ coprime to $Mp$ with $b' \equiv 1 \pmod M$ and $b' \equiv b \pmod p$, every ring automorphism $S_s$ of $K$ acting on Laurent series by applying $s$ to coefficients, and every $L$-algebra automorphism $\theta_{b'}$ of $K$ which agrees, on elements with equal Laurent series, with [`ModularCurve.baseChangeAut L (ModularCurve.diamondAut (M * p) b')`](def/ModularCurve_X1Diamond.html#L24) — the chosen $L$-linear coefficientwise extension of the chosen diamond automorphism $\langle b' \rangle$ of the $\mathbb{Q}$-function field of $X_1(Mp)$, the latter characterised by sending $p_f/p_g$ to the ratio of the $\gamma$-slashed $q$-expansions for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(Mp)$ with upper-left entry $\equiv b' \pmod{Mp}$ — one has, for all $x \in K$, the equality of Laurent series $S_s(\sigma(S_s^{-1}x)) = \sigma(\theta_{b'}x)$.
--
--   This is the rationality of the Atkin–Lehner automorphism $W_p$ over $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_p)$ in function-field form: conjugating an automorphism obeying the $W_p$ generator law by the coefficientwise Galois action of $s\zeta = \zeta^b$ produces the same automorphism composed with the diamond operator $\langle b' \rangle$, $b' \equiv b \pmod p$, $b' \equiv 1 \pmod M$. It feeds the package assembling the Atkin–Lehner involution, the diamond conjugation relation and the Galois conjugation relation on the base-changed function field of $X_1(Mp)$.
